The same-size 4B comparison reveals a 25.59\pp{} gap: \cls{} achieves 83.63\% accuracy (macro \fone{} 75.63) versus \cwm{} at 58.04\% (macro \fone{} 51.67) on seed 42. \cwm{} falls 14.92\pp{} below the majority baseline (72.96\%), indicating negative utility---a system that always predicts pass outperforms \cwm{}. A second \cwm{} seed (123) yields 59.09\% with a higher unparseable rate (67.2\% vs.\ 53.7\%), confirming the failure is not seed-specific.

The primary driver is output format compliance collapse. \cwm{} produces unparseable outputs for 53.7\% (seed 42) and 67.2\% (seed 123) of test instances, while \cls{} produces zero unparseable outputs across all configurations. The generation budget (1,024 tokens) is sufficient for the outputs required---this is not a token-budget limitation but a failure of the model to produce structured execution predictions when faced with repository-level code complexity. Among parseable \cwm{} outputs, accuracy is 42--43\%---below random chance---indicating that even well-formed \cwm{} predictions are anti-informative at this complexity level. \cls{}, producing a single binary token per test, is immune to format compliance failures by construction. Figure~\ref{fig:scaling_gap} visualizes the gap amplification from function level (1.24\pp{}) to repo level (25.59\pp{}).

For scaling context, we include \cls{} 8B results. \cls{} 8B achieves 83.52\% (seed 42) and 85.60\% (seed 123), yielding cross-size gaps of 25.48\pp{} and 26.51\pp{} against \cwm{} 4B at the corresponding seeds. Within \cls{}, the 4B$\to$8B transition at repo level shows 83.63\% $\to$ 83.52\% (seed 42), a difference within noise, suggesting that classification may be approaching a capacity ceiling at repo-level complexity or that checkpoint selection variance dominates at this scale. \cwm{} 8B training is in progress; if \cwm{} 8B performs similarly to 4B, this would confirm a generation bottleneck rather than a capacity limitation.

\paragraph{Limitations.} The same-size 4B comparison uses a single seed; \cls{} 4B seed 123 test evaluation has not been run. The validation and test sets have zero problem overlap but differ in mutation type composition, introducing potential distribution shift. All repo-level results use val-selected checkpoints, and checkpoint selection variance could contribute to observed differences.


\subsection{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\paragraph{(a) Error complementarity.}
\cls{} and \cwm{} make errors in opposite directions at function level. Among \cls{} errors (seed 42), 75.5\% are false negatives (FAIL $\to$ PASS), meaning the classifier tends to over-predict passing. Among \cwm{} errors, 83.1\% are false positives (PASS $\to$ FAIL), reflecting string-matching failures that incorrectly reject correct outputs. An oracle combination that takes the correct answer whenever either model is right achieves 90.63\% accuracy, a 3.12\pp{} improvement over \cls{} alone (87.51\%). This complementarity suggests that ensemble approaches combining classification and generation signals could yield further gains.

\paragraph{(b) CWM error mode breakdown.}
At repo level, \cwm{} output format compliance collapses. Of 9,784 test instances, 5,255 (53.7\%, seed 42) and 6,574 (67.2\%, seed 123) produce unparseable outputs. Among unparseable outputs, 76.9\% contain no execution keywords whatsoever, while 22.9\% contain ambiguous co-occurring keywords for both pass and fail. Among parseable outputs, \cwm{} achieves only 42.88\% accuracy (seed 42)---below the 50\% random baseline, indicating that even when the model produces well-formed outputs, its predictions are anti-informative. Format compliance varies across repositories: scikit-learn instances yield near-zero unparseable rates, while sphinx instances are 100\% unparseable. This per-repo variation suggests that certain code contexts---likely those with complex execution traces or domain-specific output patterns---prevent the model from generating structured responses.

\paragraph{(c) Variance compression.}
\cwm{}'s 5-seed standard deviation (0.36\pp{}) is lower than \cls{} (0.78\pp{}) and TRAJ (0.97\pp{}) at function level. This is consistent with the bottleneck hypothesis: if \cwm{} performance is capped by output-format constraints, seed-to-seed variability is compressed relative to formulations whose performance depends more on learned representations.

\paragraph{(d) Per-test precision and recall.}
At the per-test level (seed 42), \cls{} achieves fail \fone{} 87.86\% (precision 82.75\%, recall 93.64\%) and pass \fone{} 87.15\%. Of 114 problems, 48 (42.1\%) are perfectly predicted. Performance varies by mutation type: \texttt{return\_value} is easiest (97.3\%) while \texttt{off\_by\_one} is hardest (82.6\%). Multi-F2P instances with 6--10 tests reach 96.3\% accuracy, corroborating the oracle finding that per-test granularity is most valuable with multiple F2P tests.

\paragraph{(e) Majority voting.}
Majority voting ($K{=}3$) decreases accuracy from 87.51\% to 86.83\% ($-$0.68\pp{}): errors are systematic rather than stochastic, so sampling-based ensembles do not help.

\paragraph{(f) Block correlation.}
Prediction errors within the same problem are correlated: ICC $= 0.191$ ($p < 0.0001$), DEFF $= 3.97$, with 2.81$\times$ more all-correct problems than expected under i.i.d.\ errors. All statistical tests use clustered bootstrap to account for this, and the block-correlated oracle model (\S\ref{sec:oracle-results}) is the more relevant estimate for deployment.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

We compare three formulations for execution-free code repair verification, differing in output granularity and generation requirements. Given a candidate patch and its associated test suite, each formulation predicts correctness at a different level of abstraction. We then analyze the failure modes specific to generation-based verification and develop an oracle framework that characterizes when per-test granularity provides value.

\subsection{Problem Setup}
\label{sec:problem-setup}

Let $p$ denote a candidate code patch and $T = \{t_1, \ldots, t_n\}$ its associated test suite. Each test $t_i$ has a ground-truth label $y_i \in \{\textsc{Pass}, \textsc{Fail}\}$ determined by actual execution. The test suite decomposes into two disjoint subsets: \emph{fail-to-pass} tests $T_{\text{f2p}} \subseteq T$, which fail before the patch and should pass after a correct fix, and \emph{pass-to-pass} tests $T_{\text{p2p}} \subseteq T$, which pass before and should continue passing. A patch is correct if and only if all tests pass: $\forall\, t_i \in T{:}\ y_i = \textsc{Pass}$.

The verification task is to predict patch correctness without executing the code. We study three formulations that differ in prediction granularity and output format.

\subsection{Verification Formulations}
\label{sec:formulations}

We compare three formulations differing in output granularity. Let $c_p$ denote the code context (unified git diff) of patch $p$.

\paragraph{\textsc{CLS} (per-test classification).} A function $f(c_p, t_i) \rightarrow \hat{y}_i \in \{\textsc{Pass}, \textsc{Fail}\}$ predicts a single binary token per test, trained with cross-entropy loss. The patch-level verdict is obtained by conjunction: $\hat{y}_p = \bigwedge_{i=1}^{n} [\hat{y}_i = \textsc{Pass}]$. Inference cost is exactly one token per test, independent of output complexity.

\paragraph{\textsc{CWM} (output generation).} Following Code World Models~\citep{meta2025cwm}, a function $g(c_p, t_i) \rightarrow \hat{o}_i \in \Sigma^*$ generates the execution output string, trained with language modeling loss. The verdict is derived by exact match: $\hat{y}_i = \textsc{Pass}$ iff $\hat{o}_i = o_i^*$.\footnote{Exact string matching is a conservative lower bound on \cwm{} accuracy, consistent with \citet{meta2025cwm}. Semantic equivalences are not counted.} Inference cost is $\sum_{i=1}^{n} |\hat{o}_i|$ tokens, scaling with output complexity.

\paragraph{\textsc{TRAJ} (trajectory-level).} A function $h(c_p, T) \rightarrow \hat{y}_p \in \{\textsc{Resolved}, \textsc{Unresolved}\}$ predicts a single verdict for the entire patch from all tests concatenated into one input. This provides no per-test signal. We include \textsc{TRAJ} as a control: any accuracy difference between \textsc{CLS} and \textsc{TRAJ} at the patch level reflects the value of per-test decomposition, independent of the classification-vs-generation distinction.

\subsection{CWM Failure Modes}
\label{sec:failure-modes}

The generation formulation introduces three systematic failure modes absent in classification. \emph{Token-budget exhaustion}: when $|o_i^*| > B$ (generation budget), the output is truncated, guaranteeing exact-match failure regardless of prediction quality. \emph{String-value prediction errors}: even within budget, semantically equivalent outputs differing in surface form (floating-point precision, dictionary ordering, whitespace) produce false negatives, with error probability growing multiplicatively in output length. \emph{Format compliance collapse}: at repository-level complexity, the model may fail to produce structured execution predictions entirely, generating unstructured text that cannot be parsed into a verdict---even when the generation budget is sufficient. This third failure mode is qualitatively distinct: it reflects a breakdown in the model's ability to follow the output format, not a limitation of the format itself.

\textsc{CLS} bypasses all three by construction: its single-token output cannot be truncated, requires no string matching, and is always parseable. Crucially, classification does not require \emph{explicit} output prediction---the model need only perform implicit execution reasoning sufficient to discriminate pass from fail, a lower-bandwidth information-theoretic task than reproducing the exact output string. We hypothesize a \emph{complexity-dependent advantage}: both formulations perform comparably on simple code with short outputs, but \textsc{CLS} gains as output complexity triggers CWM failure modes.

\subsection{Oracle Accuracy-Utility Framework}
\label{sec:oracle}

Per-test granularity is only valuable when (a) per-test predictions are sufficiently accurate and (b) multiple tests per patch provide partially independent signals. We formalize these conditions.

Consider a buggy patch with $k = |T_{\text{f2p}}|$ fail-to-pass tests. A per-test verifier with recall $r$ detects the bug if it identifies at least one failing test: $P_{\text{detect}}(k) = 1 - (1 - r)^{k_{\text{eff}}}$,
where $k_{\text{eff}}$ is the effective number of independent tests. A trajectory-level verifier yields detection probability $a_h$ independent of $k$.

\paragraph{Block-correlation model.} Per-test predictions within the same patch are not independent. We model this using the intraclass correlation coefficient $\rho$ (ICC). The effective independent test count for a patch with $k$ F2P tests is:
%
\begin{equation}
  k_{\text{eff}} = \frac{k}{1 + (k - 1)\rho}
  \label{eq:keff}
\end{equation}
%
When $\rho = 0$, $k_{\text{eff}} = k$ (full independence); when $\rho = 1$, $k_{\text{eff}} = 1$ (per-test collapses to trajectory-level). The per-test advantage relative to a trajectory-level baseline is $\Delta(k) = P_{\text{detect}}(k) - a_h$, and the expected population advantage is:
%
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{E}[\Delta] = \sum_{k=1}^{K} P(|T_{\text{f2p}}| = k) \cdot \Delta(k)
  \label{eq:expected-advantage}
\end{equation}

This yields three predictions: (1)~at $k = 1$, $\Delta \approx 0$; (2)~$\Delta(k)$ increases with $k$ but is dampened by $\rho > 0$; (3)~$\mathbb{E}[\Delta]$ concentrates in the tail of multi-F2P-test instances. We evaluate these predictions empirically in \S\ref{sec:experiments}, including the role of P2P regression rates.
